WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.566
Endpoint security policies
aren't a higher-priority source;

2
00:00:03.566 --> 00:00:06.800
they have the same standing
as configuration profiles.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.500
In the program's library they own
every Defender, BitLocker, firewall,

4
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:18.800
account protection and LAPS setting,
and the four settings catalog IDs that

5
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.300
duplicated them, SC-002, 006, 007 and
008, are retired and never reused.

6
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:30.244
ES-001, antivirus: cloud block level
High, a fifty-second extended timeout,

7
00:00:30.244 --> 00:00:35.000
which is the maximum, and safe
samples sent automatically.

8
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.150
Sending all samples can upload
documents with organizational data,

9
00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.188
so that's the data owner's decision.

10
00:00:41.188 --> 00:00:45.103
Tamper protection needs Defender
onboarding and isn't supported

11
00:00:45.103 --> 00:00:48.876
this way on co-managed devices
until Endpoint Protection moves.

12
00:00:48.876 --> 00:00:55.317
ES-002, BitLocker: silent encryption and
a TPM PIN can't apply to the same device,

13
00:00:55.317 --> 00:01:00.450
so standard and kiosk devices
encrypt silently with TPM only,

14
00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:04.375
and Tier 0 uses TPM plus
PIN in a separate policy.

15
00:01:04.375 --> 00:01:09.772
ES-003, firewall: on an Entra-joined
device the domain profile doesn't apply,

16
00:01:09.772 --> 00:01:13.842
so management rules need a
private-profile equivalent,

17
00:01:13.842 --> 00:01:18.000
and Group Policy analytics
can't migrate firewall rules.

18
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.215
ES-004, EDR: automated investigation,

19
00:01:21.215 --> 00:01:28.287
live response and web content filtering
are set in the Defender portal, not here.

20
00:01:28.287 --> 00:01:34.190
ES-005: attack surface reduction rules
in audit for thirty days, then block;

21
00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:39.415
the PSExec and WMI rule stays off
Configuration Manager devices,

22
00:01:39.415 --> 00:01:42.125
because its client depends on WMI.

23
00:01:42.125 --> 00:01:46.759
ES-006: Credential Guard, which
needs Enterprise or Education,

24
00:01:46.759 --> 00:01:52.937
Hello for Business, and Windows LAPS with
an eight-hour post-authentication reset.

25
00:01:52.937 --> 00:01:55.238
Appendix A flags the traps.

26
00:01:55.238 --> 00:01:58.900
Account lockout maps to the
DeviceLock lockout policy.

27
00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:02.075
Audit Logon sits under
the logon-logoff path.

28
00:02:02.075 --> 00:02:05.275
A logon message breaks
Autopilot pre-provisioning.

29
00:02:05.275 --> 00:02:09.320
And Known Folder Move won't carry
folders redirected to a share:

30
00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.950
copy them first with Migration Manager.
